window.docContents[193].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-cube":{"contents":"When we first introduced the hypercube we gave two other constructions. One of these\nwas the Hamming cube hypercubeHamming: the graph on Fin n → Bool in which two\nvertices are adjacent iff their Hamming distance, computed by Mathlib's hammingDist\nfor Pi-types, is one. Having identified the graph distance in Q[n] with the Hamming\ndistance (edist_eq_hammingDist), we are now in a position to prove that this\nconstruction agrees with the one we adopted, i.e. Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n.\n\nFirst we need to identify the two vertex types, i.e. give an equiv\nHypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool). We build this by recursion on n. The base case\nholds since both types have exactly one element. In the inductive step we send\n(v, b) to the function whose first n coordinates are those of v and whose last\ncoordinate is b. Mathlib's Fin.snoc, which appends an element at the end of a\ntuple, does exactly this (and Fin.snocEquiv is Fin.snoc as an equiv).\n\n#check Fin.snoc       -- append an element at the end of an n-tuple\n#check Fin.snocEquiv  -- Fin.snoc as an equiv\n\n\nThe equiv between the two vertex types of the hypercube, HypercubeVert n and\nFin n → Bool, sending (v, b) to Fin.snoc (equivPi n v) b, so b is the last\ncoordinate./-- The equiv between the two vertex types of the hypercube, HypercubeVert n and\nFin n → Bool, sending (v, b) to Fin.snoc (equivPi n v) b, so b is the last\ncoordinate. -/\ndef HypercubeVert.equivPi : (n : ℕ) → HypercubeVert n ≃ (Fin n → Bool)\n  | 0 => Equiv.ofUnique Unit _\n  | n + 1 => (equivPi n).prodCongr (Equiv.refl _) |>.trans <|\n      (Equiv.prodComm _ _).trans <| Fin.snocEquiv fun _ => _\n\n\nUnfolding the equiv one step is definitionally Fin.snoc, and marking this as\n@[simp] lets simp compute with HypercubeVert.equivPi recursively.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.equivPi_succ_apply {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b : Bool) :\n    equivPi (n + 1) (v, b) = Fin.snoc (v.equivPi n) b := rfl\n\n\nTo transfer distances along the equiv we need to know how Mathlib's hammingDist\ninteracts with Fin.snoc: appending a coordinate to each of two vertices increases\ntheir Hamming distance by one exactly when the appended coordinates differ. (Note how\nthis mirrors the recursion in HypercubeVert.hammingDist.) To prove this we write\nthe Hamming distance as a sum of indicators, using Finset.card_filter, and split\noff the last coordinate with Fin.sum_univ_castSucc.\n\nlemma _root_.hammingDist_snoc {n : ℕ} {β : Type*} [DecidableEq β] (x y : Fin n → β)\n    (a b : β) :\n    hammingDist (Fin.snoc x a : Fin (n + 1) → β) (Fin.snoc y b) =\n      hammingDist x y + if a = b then 0 else 1 := by\n  simp [hammingDist, Finset.card_filter, Fin.sum_univ_castSucc]\n\n\nThe Hamming distance of HypercubeVert n vertices agrees with the Hamming distance\nof the corresponding functions Fin n → Bool, by induction on n.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma HypercubeVert.hammingDist_equivPi {n : ℕ} (u v : HypercubeVert n) :\n    _root_.hammingDist (u.equivPi n) (v.equivPi n) = u.hammingDist v := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero => rfl\n  | succ n ih =>\n    obtain ⟨u, b⟩ := u\n    obtain ⟨v, c⟩ := v\n    simp [hammingDist_snoc, ih]\n\n\nAdjacency in the Hamming cube is Hamming distance one, by definition.\n\n@[simp]\nlemma hypercubeHamming_adj {n : ℕ} {x y : Fin n → Bool} :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).Adj x y ↔ hammingDist x y = 1 := Iff.rfl\n\n\nWe can now give the graph isomorphism: the underlying equiv is\nHypercubeVert.equivPi, and it respects adjacency since both adjacency relations say\nexactly \"Hamming distance one\".\n\nThe hypercube Q[n] (the box product construction) is isomorphic to the Hamming\ncube construction hypercubeHamming n on Fin n → Bool./-- The hypercube Q[n] (the box product construction) is isomorphic to the Hamming\ncube construction hypercubeHamming n on Fin n → Bool. -/\ndef hypercubeIsoHamming (n : ℕ) : Q[n] ≃g hypercubeHamming n where\n  toEquiv := HypercubeVert.equivPi n\n  map_rel_iff' := by\n    simp [hypercube_adj_iff_hammingDist_eq_one]\n\n\nThis isomorphism is a bridge between the two constructions: results proved for\nQ[n] by induction transport to hypercubeHamming, while statements that need\nthe coordinates of a vertex, such as identifying the automorphism group of the\nhypercube, are more naturally proved for hypercubeHamming and transported back.\n\nTransporting edist_eq_hammingDist along hypercubeIsoHamming shows that the graph\ndistance in the Hamming cube is exactly the Hamming distance.\n\nlemma edist_hypercubeHamming {n : ℕ} (x y : Fin n → Bool) :\n    (hypercubeHamming n).edist x y = hammingDist x y := by\n  obtain ⟨u, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective x\n  obtain ⟨v, rfl⟩ := (HypercubeVert.equivPi n).surjective y\n  exact ((hypercubeIsoHamming n).edist_eq u v).trans (by simp [edist_eq_hammingDist])\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory","header":"12.5. The Hypercube and the Hamming Cube","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-the-hypercube-and-the-hamming-cube"},"/Connectivity/#section-connectivity":{"contents":"In Mathlib connectedness is defined in two steps: a graph is preconnected\n(Preconnected) if there is a walk between every pair of vertices, and a graph is\nconnected (Connected) if it is preconnected and nonempty. (Recall that existence\nof a walk between a pair of vertices is Reachable which we saw in\nReachability.)\n\nopen Walk\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n#source Reachable     -- def Reachable (u v : V) : Prop := Nonempty (G.Walk u v)\n#source Preconnected  -- def Preconnected : Prop := ∀ u v : V, G.Reachable u v\n#source Connected     -- structure Connected : Prop where\n                      --   protected preconnected : G.Preconnected\n                      --   protected [nonempty : Nonempty V]\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Connectivity","header":"10.1. Connectivity","id":"/Connectivity/#section-connectivity"},"/Theorems/#section-hall-s-marriage-theorem":{"contents":"Let G be bipartite with parts p₁ and p₂ (IsBipartiteWith).\nHall's condition says that every finite set s ⊆ p₁ has at least |s| neighbours.\nHall's theorem P. Hall, On representatives of subsets, J. London Math. Soc. 10 (1935), no. 1, 26–30. says that this condition implies that G has a\nmatching whose vertices include all of p₁. (The converse is easy: the partners of\nthe vertices in s are distinct neighbours of s.)\n\nA bipartite graph with a subset s and its open neighbourhood N(s)\n\n#check exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nNotice that V need not be finite: Mathlib only assumes that G is locally finite,\nand we will see that even this is more than the proof needs. Hall's condition is\nstated with Set.ncard, which is 0 on infinite sets, so it holds trivially for\ninfinite s and only finite sets need to be checked.\n\n#check Set.Infinite.ncard -- s.Infinite → s.ncard = 0\n\n\nexample {s : Set V} (hs : s.Infinite) :\n    s.ncard ≤ (⋃ x ∈ s, G.neighborSet x).ncard := by\n  simp [hs.ncard]\n\n\nFor a perfect matching, Mathlib asks for Hall's condition for every set s of\nvertices, which may contain vertices from both sides.\n\n#check exists_isPerfectMatching_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nBoth theorems come from the statement about systems of distinct representatives: a\nfamily of finite sets t : ι → Finset α has an injective choice function iff every\nunion of k of the sets has at least k elements. Mathlib states it for any index\ntype ι. For infinite ι it follows from the finite case by a compactness argument:\neach finite set of indices has at least one injective choice and only finitely many,\nand nonempty_sections_of_finite_inverse_system makes the choices agree.\n\n#check Finset.all_card_le_biUnion_card_iff_exists_injective\n\n\nMathlib applies it to the finsets G.neighborFinset x with x ∈ p₁, and it is to\nform these that it assumes [G.LocallyFinite]. For a perfect matching it applies it\nto all of V, which is why the condition is stated for all sets of vertices. This\ngives injections p₁ → p₂ and p₂ → p₁ along edges. If V is finite then\n|p₁| = |p₂|, and either one is already a perfect matching. In general Mathlib\ncombines them using a relational form of the Schröder–Bernstein theorem\n(Function.Embedding.schroeder_bernstein_of_rel), which yields a bijection p₁ → p₂\nalong edges.\n\n#check exists_bijective_of_forall_ncard_le\n\n\nLocal finiteness cannot be dropped from the classical theorem. An example is a\nsubdivided star: a star with each edge subdivided by a new vertex. Its vertices are\na centre and two copies of an index type ι, so we reuse the vertex type\nOption (ι ⊕ ι) of the Mycielskian and its notation: the centre none is adjacent\nto every iʳ, and each iˡ is adjacent only to iʳ.\n\nThe subdivided star on ι: the centre none is adjacent to every iʳ, and\neach iˡ is adjacent only to iʳ./-- The *subdivided star* on ι: the centre none is adjacent to every iʳ, and\neach iˡ is adjacent only to iʳ. -/\n@[simps]\ndef subdividedStar (ι : Type*) : SimpleGraph (Option (ι ⊕ ι)) where\n  Adj x y := match x, y with\n    | none, _ʳ | _ʳ, none => True\n    | iˡ, jʳ | jʳ, iˡ => i = j\n    | _, _ => False\n  symm := ⟨by rintro (_ | _ | _) (_ | _ | _) h <;> simp_all [eq_comm]⟩\n  loopless := ⟨by rintro (_ | _ | _) <;> simp⟩\n\n\nIn subdividedStar ℕ, every finite set of the vertices none, 0ˡ, 1ˡ, … has at\nleast as many neighbours as elements, but no matching covers them all: whichever jʳ\nis matched to none, the vertex jˡ is left unmatched, since jʳ is its only\nneighbour. In particular, a subdivided star has no perfect matching.\n\nThe subdividedStar ℕ. A matching (red) covering none (drawn as ∗) leaves a vertex (orange) unmatched\n\nA subdivided star has no perfect matching./-- A subdivided star has no perfect matching. -/\nlemma not_exists_isPerfectMatching_subdividedStar {ι : Type*} :\n    ¬∃ M : (subdividedStar ι).Subgraph, M.IsPerfectMatching := by\n  rintro ⟨M, hM, hspan⟩\n  -- none is matched to a right vertex jʳ\n  obtain ⟨w, hw, -⟩ := hM (hspan none)\n  obtain _ | j | j := w\n  · simpa using M.adj_sub hw\n  · simpa using M.adj_sub hw\n  -- jˡ is matched to jʳ as well\n  obtain ⟨u, hu, -⟩ := hM (hspan jˡ)\n  obtain _ | k | k := u\n  · simpa using M.adj_sub hu\n  · simpa using M.adj_sub hu\n  obtain rfl : j = k := by simpa using M.adj_sub hu\n  simpa using hM.eq_of_adj_right hw hu\n\n\nIn Lean, though, this graph fails Hall's condition for the set {none}: none has\ninfinitely many neighbours, so the Set.ncard of its neighbourhood is 0.\n\nexample : ¬({none} : Set (Option (ℕ ⊕ ℕ))).ncard ≤\n    (⋃ x ∈ ({none} : Set _), (subdividedStar ℕ).neighborSet x).ncard := by\n  -- the neighbours of none are the right vertices iʳ\n  have h : ((subdividedStar ℕ).neighborSet none).Infinite :=\n    Set.infinite_of_injective_forall_mem mycielskian.right_injective (by simp)\n  simp [h.ncard]\n\n\nSo Lean's statement of Hall's condition already forces every vertex of p₁ to have\nfinite degree. exists_isMatching_of_forall_ncard_le assumes [G.LocallyFinite],\nwhich asks the same of p₂, but its proof does not need this: it picks partners for\nthe vertices of p₁ from their neighbourhoods and never looks at the neighbourhood\nof a vertex in p₂. Mathlib's relational form of the theorem on systems of distinct\nrepresentatives asks for exactly this one-sided finiteness: each a : α must be\nrelated to finitely many elements of β, and nothing is assumed about β.\n\n#check Fintype.all_card_le_rel_image_card_iff_exists_injective\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Matchings","header":"13.1.1. Hall's Marriage Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-hall-s-marriage-theorem"}});